This is an important Java concurrency question for a 10-year experienced Java developer. The interviewer is not just checking whether you know the difference between these two APIs, but whether you understand thread safety, locking mechanisms, concurrency, performance, and real-world usage.

Let's approach it as an experienced Java developer answering in an interview.
1. First, understand both approaches
A. Collections.synchronizedMap()
Collections.synchronizedMap() is a wrapper that makes an existing Map thread-safe by synchronizing its methods.
Map<String, Integer> map =
        Collections.synchronizedMap(new HashMap<>());

map.put("Java", 10);
map.put("Spring", 20);
map.get("Java");


How it works:
- It wraps the original HashMap.
- It uses synchronization to protect individual Map operations.
- Only one thread at a time can execute a synchronized Map operation.
- Other threads must wait until the current operation releases the lock.
- It uses a common mutex for the wrapped Map.
B. ConcurrentHashMap
ConcurrentHashMap is specifically designed for concurrent access by multiple threads.
Map<String, Integer> map = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

map.put("Java", 10);
map.put("Spring", 20);
map.get("Java");


How it works:
- Multiple threads can read and update the Map concurrently.
- It uses CAS (Compare-And-Swap) and fine-grained synchronization where needed.
- It does not lock the entire Map for every operation.
- It provides better concurrency under typical multi-threaded workloads.
Note: Modern Java implementations of ConcurrentHashMap use CAS and synchronized locking on individual bins when required, rather than the old Java 7 segment-based locking design.
