Important historical point:
- Java 7: ConcurrentHashMap used Segment-based locking.
- Java 8 onwards: It moved to a CAS-based approach with synchronized locking at the bin level when necessary.
- Modern Java versions retain this general design.
This is a good point to mention because some interviewers deliberately ask about Java 7 versus Java 8 implementations.
4. Important difference: Iteration behavior
This is one of the most commonly missed interview points.
synchronizedMap iteration
You must manually synchronize on the Map while iterating.
Map<String, Integer> map =
        Collections.synchronizedMap(new HashMap<>());

map.put("Java", 10);
map.put("Spring", 20);

synchronized (map) {
    for (Map.Entry<String, Integer> entry : map.entrySet()) {
        System.out.println(
            entry.getKey() + " : " + entry.getValue()
        );
    }
}


Why?

Because the iterator itself is not automatically protected by the synchronized wrapper. Without external synchronization, concurrent modifications can cause inconsistent iteration or a ConcurrentModificationException.
ConcurrentHashMap iteration
Map<String, Integer> map = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

map.put("Java", 10);
map.put("Spring", 20);

for (Map.Entry<String, Integer> entry : map.entrySet()) {
    System.out.println(
        entry.getKey() + " : " + entry.getValue()
    );
}


No external synchronization is required.

Its iterators are weakly consistent:
- They do not throw ConcurrentModificationException just because another thread modifies the Map.
- They may reflect some modifications made during iteration.
- They are not guaranteed to represent one fixed snapshot of the Map.
5. Important difference: Null key and null value
// synchronizedMap
Map<String, Integer> map1 =
        Collections.synchronizedMap(new HashMap<>());

map1.put(null, 10);       // Allowed
map1.put("Java", null);   // Allowed

// ConcurrentHashMap
Map<String, Integer> map2 = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

map2.put(null, 10);       // NullPointerException
map2.put("Java", null);   // NullPointerException


Why doesn't ConcurrentHashMap allow null?

Because in a concurrent environment, a get(key) returning null should unambiguously mean that no mapping is currently available for that key.

Allowing null values would make it difficult to distinguish between an absent mapping and a mapping explicitly containing null.
6. Real-world scenario: Atomic operations
This is where an experienced Java developer should demonstrate deeper knowledge.

Suppose you want to increment a counter stored in a Map.
Using synchronizedMap
Map<String, Integer> map =
        Collections.synchronizedMap(new HashMap<>());

map.put("count", 0);

synchronized (map) {
    Integer count = map.get("count");
    map.put("count", count + 1);
}


Why synchronize explicitly?

Because get() and put() are individually synchronized, but the entire read-modify-write sequence is not atomic.

Without external synchronization, two threads could read the same value and overwrite each other's updates.
Using ConcurrentHashMap
ConcurrentHashMap<String, Integer> map =
        new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

map.put("count", 0);

map.compute("count", (key, value) -> value + 1);


Here, compute() performs the update atomically for the specified key.

Another useful example:
map.putIfAbsent("Java", 10);

map.computeIfAbsent("Spring", key -> 20);

map.merge("count", 1, Integer::sum);


These methods are extremely useful in concurrent programming.

Interview point: Thread-safe individual methods do not automatically make a sequence of multiple method calls thread-safe. Use atomic Map operations or appropriate synchronization.
7. Real-world use cases
Scenario	Recommended approach
Simple legacy application with low concurrency	synchronizedMap
Shared cache accessed by multiple threads	ConcurrentHashMap
High-throughput microservices	ConcurrentHashMap
Configuration Map with synchronized access	synchronizedMap can be sufficient
Concurrent counters or frequency maps	ConcurrentHashMap with merge/compute
Need null keys and values with synchronized access	synchronizedMap

For example, in a Spring Boot application, suppose multiple requests access a shared in-memory cache.
@Service
public class EmployeeCacheService {

    private final ConcurrentHashMap<Long, String> cache =
            new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    public void addEmployee(Long id, String name) {
        cache.put(id, name);
    }

    public String getEmployee(Long id) {
        return cache.get(id);
    }

    public void removeEmployee(Long id) {
        cache.remove(id);
    }
}


Multiple HTTP request threads can access this shared cache concurrently without requiring you to synchronize every operation manually.

For a production distributed application, remember that ConcurrentHashMap is local to one JVM. It is not a distributed cache.
